<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Amazing Community.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write emails, letters, or texts describing past, present, or future events.<br>interactive: Edit short texts with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write short emails describing community events and activities using sentence starters (e.g., 'Today, I went to...' 'My favorite part was...').<br>Can draft letters describing upcoming events (e.g., 'The school has organized a community clean-up for this weekend.').<br>Can edit and revise with peers short descriptions of community events (e.g., 'Add more details about the market.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or photos of various community places (e.g., park, market, school, library) and community roles (e.g., teacher, farmer, police officer). Students quickly write down one word that comes to mind for each image on their mini-whiteboards or notebooks. Then, students share their words aloud in a quick round-robin. The teacher writes key words like 'community', 'event', 'park', 'school', 'market' on the board, emphasizing stress in compound nouns like 'neighborhood'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing two simple sentences on the board about a community event, using target grammar and vocabulary. Example 1 (past): "Last year, our <b>community</b> had a fun <b>festival</b> at the <b>park</b>. My <b>family</b> <b>enjoyed</b> the <b>delicious</b> food." Example 2 (present/future): "This weekend, the <b>school</b> has organized a <b>community</b> clean-up <b>event</b>. We <b>need to</b> <b>volunteer</b>." The teacher orally highlights the past simple (`had`, `enjoyed`) and present simple (`has organized`, `need to`) and points to the time expressions (`Last year`, `This weekend`). The teacher also practices the vowel sound /ʊ/ in 'cook' and /uː/ in 'school' by saying the words clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was the festival in the first sentence happening now or in the past?" (Expected answer: In the past.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the clean-up event happening today or in the future?" (Expected answer: In the future / This weekend.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, fictional email from a student to a friend describing a community market and a planned community garden project. The email is projected on the board or provided as a handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Email Example Text:</b><br>
                <i style="text-align: justify;">Subject: Guess what happened at our market!</i><br>
                <i style="text-align: justify;">Hi Laura,</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i style="text-align: justify;">Last Saturday, our <b>community market</b> was amazing! Many <b>vendors</b> came. My mom and I <b>enjoyed</b> the <b>fresh</b> fruits and <b>delicious</b> food. I saw Mr. Juan, the <b>farmer</b>, selling his vegetables. He has worked as a <b>farmer</b> for twenty years! The <b>musician</b> played some <b>traditional</b> songs. It was so much fun!</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i style="text-align: justify;">Also, the <b>school</b> needs to <b>build</b> a new community garden next month. We <b>can volunteer</b>! <b>Should</b> we go together?</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i style="text-align: justify;">Talk soon, Ana</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student writes short texts and emails to describe community events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is Ana writing about in her email?" (Expected answer: A community market and a new garden.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Ana's mom cooked food at the market." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> "Match the event with the time: 'Community Market' (Last Saturday), 'Community Garden' (Next month)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using Present Simple, Past Simple with time expressions (e.g., `yesterday`, `last month`, `every day`), and Present Perfect. Vocabulary: `community`, `event`, `park`, `neighborhood`, `school`, `teacher`, `farmer`, `vendor`, `artist`, `cook`, `enjoy`, `celebrate`, `volunteer`, `delicious`, `friendly`, `can`, `should`, `need to`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences using the correct tense (past simple, present simple, or present perfect) and target vocabulary from a word bank.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Yesterday, our _________ (school/market) _________ (celebrate/celebrated) the new library."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The _________ (police officer/gardener) _________ (work/has worked) at the station for five years."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Every year, the _________ (community/family) _________ (gather/gathers) at the _________ (park/library) for the festival."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What _________ (do/does) people _________ (cook/cooks) at the market?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Sentence Building):</b> Students work individually to write sentences describing community events or roles based on provided prompts, ensuring they use different tenses and time expressions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompts:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The school / organize / clean-up / this weekend." (Expected: The school will organize a clean-up this weekend.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My neighbor / be / a police officer / for ten years." (Expected: My neighbor has been a police officer for ten years.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People / enjoy / the festival / last month." (Expected: People enjoyed the festival last month.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I / volunteer / at the library / every Saturday." (Expected: I volunteer at the library every Saturday.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Sentence Expansion:</b> In pairs, students exchange their sentences from activity 2. Each student adds one adjective (e.g., `friendly`, `engaging`, `delicious`) or one specific time expression (`last month`, `every day`) to one of their partner's sentences. This introduces a basic form of peer editing and sentence enrichment. For example, if a partner wrote "The school organized a clean-up this weekend," the other student might add: "The *friendly* school organized a clean-up this weekend." or "The school organized a clean-up *before the festival* this weekend."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "My Community Event Email"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students write a short email (5-7 sentences) to a friend, describing a community event. They can choose to write about:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A past event they enjoyed (e.g., a festival, a market visit).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A present event or ongoing activity (e.g., what someone in their community does as a `job`, like a `teacher` or `carpenter`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A future event the `community` or `school` has organized (e.g., a `clean-up`, a new `library` opening).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students must use at least three different time expressions (e.g., `last month`, `every day`, `next week`) and at least five words from the target vocabulary list (e.g., `community`, `park`, `festival`, `delicious`, `volunteer`). They should use sentence starters like: "Today, I went to...", "My favorite part was...", "The school has organized...", "I have worked as a...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in describing events, collaborative interaction (if students choose to write about a shared past event), and communicative task completion (informing a friend about a community event).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Peer Editing Checklist</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students exchange their emails with a peer. They use a simple checklist to review their partner's email. The checklist includes:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my friend describe a community event? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my friend use words like 'community', 'park', 'festival'? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my friend use time words like 'last year', 'this weekend', 'every day'? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I understand the email easily? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Suggest one thing to add or improve (e.g., 'Add more details about the market.' or 'Use a different adjective for the food.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the email.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of at least 5 target vocabulary words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of present simple, past simple, and/or present perfect tenses in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of time expressions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to provide constructive peer feedback.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word I used today about my community?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I describe a past, present, or future event in my email?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about my community was easy, and what was challenging?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I make my descriptions of community events even better next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., specific activities, student engagement with peer editing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support students' writing skills further? (e.g., more sentence starters, explicit grammar review for past perfect).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively use the different tenses (present simple, past simple, present perfect) and the target vocabulary?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The writing skills developed today (describing community events, roles, and activities using varied tenses) will be directly applied in Lesson 5, where students will create a larger written product for their 21st Century Skills Project, such as a community guide or a descriptive text for a poster about their amazing community.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>